{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 46, :comments ["<b>And Jacob said to his brothers</b> — to the brothers of Laban who are mentioned, who came with him, for he did not wish to say thus to his father-in-law, because he behaved toward him with honor. And likewise, \"and he called to his brothers to eat bread\" (Genesis 31:54) — to the brothers of Laban, and he did not call to Laban, out of honor, as though everything is in his authority and everything is his. And similar to it is \"and Pharaoh said to his brothers, what is your occupation\" (Genesis 47:3). And Rashi explained \"his brothers\" as the brothers of Jacob, for those who came with Laban are his loved ones and friends. And it is possible that what it says concerning Laban, \"and he took his brothers with him\" (Genesis 31:23), refers to his relatives of his family from the seed of Nahor the brother of Abraham, and he did so because he did not wish to bring against Jacob foreign men, lest they contend with him by force, or covet and plunder and steal from what is his. Now these are relatives to Jacob just as to Laban; therefore they are called brothers to both of them. And some say (Piskta Zutrata above 29:4) that he called them \"brothers\" by way of etiquette, just as he said to the shepherds (ibid.), \"My brothers, where are you from?\" And in Bereshit Rabbah (74:13) they said: \"And Jacob said to his brothers\" — these are his sons, for he would call them, in an expression of honor, \"my brothers.\" But regarding \"and he called to his brothers to eat bread,\" they did not explain. And the correct interpretation is what I wrote at the beginning." "<b>And they ate there upon the heap</b> — they ate there a little as a remembrance, or it is the way of those entering into a covenant that both of them eat from one bread, for love and fellowship, and after they had entered into the oath and the covenant, he slaughtered a sacrifice for them and made a great feast for them. And it is possible that the meaning of \"and they ate there upon the heap\" refers to the sacrifice mentioned below, for he said to them, gather stones and let us make a heap to eat upon it, and it shall be a witness when we enter into the covenant, and afterward they ate a sacrifice upon it. And the meaning of \"and they ate bread\" is that he made them a great meal to lodge with him — not merely a banquet."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "Ramban's opening reading identifies 'his brothers' as Laban's kinsmen mentioned in v. 23, then surveys Rashi, Piskta Zutrata, and Bereshit Rabbah before affirming his first interpretation.", 1 "The 'sacrifice mentioned below' refers to ויזבח זבח in v. 54; Ramban discusses the sequence of the heap, the covenant meal, and the sacrificial feast within vv. 46–54."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:38:20.780371776Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}